          "type": "turn",
          "question": "When a reference note names a root like K21, what do I look for to get to the specific code?",
          "answer": "The search results don't cover the K21 code or its section of the classification, so I can't tell you what the specific codes under K21 are.\n\nThe retrieved material does describe how ICD-10-CM codes are built, which may help you read the reference note:\n\n- **Chapter and block.** The first letter marks the chapter, and the code ranges mark blocks within it. Chapter 13 (musculoskeletal) uses M00 to M99, for example, and groups related conditions into blocks such as M15 to M19 for osteoarthritis (coding-icd10-chapter-13-musculoskeletal).\n- **Site and side.** In the musculoskeletal chapter, the site and laterality (right, left, or unspecified) are part of the code itself, so the digits after the root often encode them (coding-icd10-chapter-13-musculoskeletal).\n- **Decimal digits.** Digits after the decimal point add detail. For example, E11.9 is a type 2 diabetes code without complications (coding-type-2-diabetes-e11).\n- **Notes that change the choice.** Look for \"Excludes1\", \"Excludes2\", \"code first\", \"use additional code\", and \"code also\" notes. They can require a second code or rule out a pairing (coding-icd10-cm-code-structure-and-conventions).\n- **Check the year.** Codes can be added, changed, or retired, so a code valid in one year may not be valid in another. Confirm any code against a current official ICD-10-CM file (coding-icd10-cm-versus-who-icd10).\n\nTo find the exact K21 subcodes, use the current official ICD-10-CM tabular list, or ask the person who wrote the reference note. A medical coder can also confirm the right code for a specific record.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
